From 02e6cce7c5ba371c87305c464ae6ca8aa1334958 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:00:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Show the welcome switches as Start will save them

When Android kills the browser while the welcome is in the background, coming
back recreates the activity, builds the welcome again from Choices, and then
restores the saved view hierarchy over it. Every row of the welcome is the same
layout, so every row carries a switch with the same id; the rows that open a
chooser keep theirs hidden and unchecked. The restore hands each of those ids
the state saved last, which is the hidden switch in the Updates row, and the
Ad blocker switch turns off while Start still saves it on. A user who reads the
screen and leaves it alone gets the opposite of what they saw; one who switches
it "back on" turns the blocker off.

It happens on any path that restores the hierarchy: process death behind the
welcome, and a relaunch of an activity that started while the phone was
locked. alpha.3 does it too.

The overlay is now left out of the activity's saved state. Choices is what
Start writes, and it is what the rows are built from, so after a restore each
row shows what Start will write. A choice made before the process died is
lost along with Choices, as it already was for the rows that open a chooser.
Turning off saving on the two visible switches is not enough: the hidden ones
still save, and a restore does not ask the receiving view whether it saves.
---
 .../org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareWelcomeOverlay.java | 5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

diff --git a/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareWelcomeOverlay.java b/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareWelcomeOverlay.java
index 24c3f4b8e6b..54a9478cd10 100644
--- a/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareWelcomeOverlay.java
+++ b/chrome/android/java/src/org/chromium/chrome/browser/bare/BareWelcomeOverlay.java
@@ -117,6 +117,11 @@ public class BareWelcomeOverlay {
         View overlay =
                 LayoutInflater.from(activity)
                         .inflate(R.layout.bare_welcome_overlay, root, /* attachToRoot= */ false);
+        // Every row is the same layout, so its views share ids, and a restored activity would
+        // hand each of them the state saved for the last row: a hidden, unchecked switch. The
+        // overlay is rebuilt from Choices whenever it is shown, so keep it out of the activity's
+        // saved state altogether.
+        overlay.setSaveFromParentEnabled(false);
         root.addView(overlay);
 
         View circle = overlay.findViewById(R.id.bare_welcome_circle);
